feat(mcp): require a repository-scoped credential on the lifecycle listener (#264)

Nothing authenticated a caller on the identity-lifecycle listener, so any
process that could reach it could mint a run — doc 04's AB-13 and AB-15 — and
the deployment that turned the listener split on published it with six tools
behind it and no check at all.

One transport-level check now sits in front of the whole admin handler. The
credential is a short-lived ES256 token carrying `iss`, `aud`, `repo`, `iat`,
`nbf`, `exp` and `jti`, and nothing that identifies an account: the claim set is
closed and the decode is strict, so a token carrying `sub`, an account id, an
email or a display name is refused rather than tolerated. It is verified offline
against a JWK set read once from a file, never fetched, so this system gains no
dependency on whatever issues it.

Every credential failure — absent, malformed, expired, not yet valid, wrong
audience, wrong issuer, unknown key id, bad signature — is one byte-identical
401 before the MCP session layer: no handler runs, nothing is appended, no
idempotency key is claimed and SPIRE is never reached. The reasons are kept for
`innsegl admin-credential verify`, which an operator runs on their own machine.

Per tool the credential's repository must match: register_agent's argument,
describe_workspace's derived repository, observe_session's derived workspace,
and for record_event / retire_agent / observe_tool_call the run's own registered
repository — answered exactly as a run that does not exist, because a run id is
public in every Agent-Run trailer. observe_session stop answers another
repository's session exactly as one this deployment never saw.

`serve` refuses to start when the listener is enabled and the key set is absent,
and refuses a key set with no listener. Readiness reports `admin_credential:
enforced|absent`, on the health listener only. The agent listener is untouched
and single-listener mode requires nothing.

The credential is a header, deliberately outside the idempotency fingerprint:
that digest is the canonical form of the tool and its arguments and nothing
else, so a replay under a different token returns the original result.

No tool name, error class, event type, field, enum or source changes.

Drove it: MCP-087 (one byte-identical refusal), MCP-088 (a credential for one
repository against another), MCP-089 (a run-scoped mismatch answered as a
nonexistent run), MCP-090 (unknown key id, rotation with overlap, and the
start-up refusal), MCP-091 (the credential in no log), MCP-092 (the agent
listener and single-listener mode unchanged), MCP-093 (outside the idempotency
fingerprint). #264 named MCP-078/079/081/082/084; RM-151..RM-157 took those in
the interval and doc 07 carries them, so the block was reallocated from the
measured next free id.

